NNAAMMEE
     aattqq — display the at(1) job queue

SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
     aattqq [--ccnnvv] [--qq _q_u_e_u_e] [_n_a_m_e _._._.]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     aattqq displays the queue of jobs, created by the at(1) command, which are
     currently awaiting execution.  Unless the user is the superuser, only the
     user's own jobs will be displayed.  With no flags, the queue is sorted in
     the order that the jobs will be executed.

     The options are as follows:

     --cc         Sort the queue by the time that the jobs were submitted (cre‐
                ated).  By default, aattqq will sort the queue by the time that
                the jobs will run.

     --nn         Only print the total number of files that are currently in the
                queue.

     --qq _q_u_e_u_e   Restrict output to jobs in the specified _q_u_e_u_e.  A queue des‐
                ignation consists of a single letter.  Valid queue designa‐
                tions range from aa to zz and AA to ZZ.  The cc queue is the de‐
                fault for at(1) and the EE queue for batch(1).  By default, aattqq
                will display jobs in all queues.

     --vv         Jobs that have completed but have not yet been removed are
                also displayed.

     If a name(s) is provided, only those files belonging to that user(s) are
     displayed.

FFIILLEESS
     /var/cron/atjobs  directory containing job files

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     at(1), atrm(1), cron(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     The aattqq command appeared in 4.3BSD.

